S[8]=dict(lbl="How will technology change demands?|How will technology change resources?|Is flexibility a resource or a demand?|What does JD-R predict?|What does COR predict?|What does the evidence so far show?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What do the two theories predict for jobs shaped by automation and AI?",
 "The second half of the article uses both theories to look ahead. The World Economic Forum expects automation to replace routine tasks and to make digital skills, data analysis, critical thinking, creativity, emotional intelligence and lifelong learning more important. What happens to demands and resources?",
 ["Three ways technology changes demands","How resources may change","Two scenarios: JD-R's and COR's","What the evidence so far shows"]),
CARD("Three changes: the nature of demands shifts to cognitive, the change itself is demanding, and there are by-products.",
 "The author draws on Demerouti (2022):"+UL([("1","<b>Nature:</b> technology takes over handling objects and information, leaving people the cognitively heavy parts; physical demands may drop."),("2","<b>The change itself:</b> new routines, training and asking for technical support raise workload and job insecurity."),("3","<b>By-products:</b> new tasks that clash with old work design, technology that intrudes into private life (work–family conflict), more surveillance and more administration.")])+"<i>Our example:</i> robots pick and pack, while warehouse staff monitor dashboards and solve exceptions.",
 "technology does not remove demands; it changes them, and the transition adds its own.",
 "what happens to resources."),
CARD("It depends on how technology is introduced (Parker &amp; Grote, 2022).",
 "Technology can increase or decrease autonomy, skill use, feedback and social contact; it \"can replace dull, dangerous, and dirty tasks while simultaneously increasing the standardization of tasks\" (p. 7). Two resources are most likely to grow: opportunities for development and learning, because complex technology demands new skills and so more training, and flexibility in time and place. <i>Our example:</i> the same scheduling app can give nurses more say over their shifts, or take that say away.",
 "the effect of technology on resources is a design choice, not fate.",
 "a trap about flexibility."),
CARD("It depends on who leads it: flexibility chosen by the employee is a resource, flexibility imposed by the employer is a demand.",
 "Employee-led flexibility: choosing your own start and end time. Employer-led flexibility: overtime, imposed hours, time restrictions. <i>Our example:</i> \"work whenever suits you\" versus \"be available whenever we call\".",
 "an exam option saying \"flexible working times are always a resource\" is wrong.",
 "the first scenario."),
CARD("Provide adequate resources that match the new demands.",
 "From JD-R: when people work with robots and AI, give them, for example, transparency and autonomy, and ask whether social resources will be kept or strengthened, so the need for relatedness is met alongside competence. <i>Our example:</i> explaining how an AI tool makes its suggestions, and letting staff decide when to follow them.",
 "JD-R's scenario is hopeful, as long as resources keep up with the new demands.",
 "the second scenario."),
CARD("A \"bleak picture\" (p. 7): people with few resources, such as low-skilled employees, precarious jobholders and people in flexible employment, are most likely to lose.",
 "COR predicts that those with fewer resources are more vulnerable to loss and less able to gain. Hence the author's strong point: responsibility should not be pushed onto employees alone; organizations, authorities and policymakers must design jobs that satisfy basic human needs (autonomy, competence, relatedness; Deci &amp; Ryan, 2000). Organizations should redesign the whole system, involve users, give resources for new demands and support crafting; policymakers should protect jobs, safe hours, income and privacy.",
 "COR adds a fairness warning to job design.",
 "what has actually happened so far?"),
CARD("Mixed: job characteristics improved over decades, but robotization brought both gains and job losses.",
 "<b>Study 1:</b> Wegman et al. (2016), a cross-temporal meta-analysis (combining studies across decades). <i>What they found:</i> from 1975 to 2010, skill variety, task identity, task significance and autonomy all rose. <b>Report 2:</b> Bréchemier et al. (2016), a consultancy report on robotization in car manufacturing (1975 to 1990). <i>What they found:</i> 40% more production, 20% fewer accidents and 45% more executives, but also a 45% smaller workforce. <i>In plain words:</i> the same technology made work better for some and removed it for others.",
 "technology is neither good nor bad for well-being in itself: it depends on how jobs are redesigned around it.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Technology changes demands (more cognitive, change-related, by-products) and makes resources a design choice; JD-R says match them, COR warns about the vulnerable.",
 [("Demands?","Shift to cognitive; the change itself; by-products (work–family conflict, surveillance, administration)."),("Resources?","Depends on introduction; learning and flexibility likely to grow."),("Flexibility?","Employee-led = resource; employer-led = demand."),("JD-R scenario?","Provide matching resources (transparency, autonomy)."),("COR scenario?","Bleak for low-skilled, precarious and flexible workers."),("Evidence?","Better job characteristics 1975–2010; robotization: more output, fewer accidents, 45% fewer workers.")],
 ["According to COR, which workers are most at risk when technology transforms jobs, and why?","Classify: overtime imposed by the employer (a demand)","A case: robots take over packing, staff now monitor screens. Which change in demands? (nature shifts to cognitive)"])],
 guides=G("Press <b>🧑 Human-centred design</b> and watch the dials for demands and resources.","Press <b>⚙️ Technology-first</b> and compare.","<b>Tap any part</b> for the explanation; <b>↺ Reset</b> to start again."),
 gguide=G("A logistics company introduces new technology. Read the consequence on the card.","<b>Classify it</b> the way the article does: a change in demands, or a resource or demand from flexibility, or a learning resource.","Read why, then go to the next consequence."))

S[9]=dict(lbl="What should researchers study next?|Which design principles does Table 3 name?|How can organizations add resources?|How can organizations reduce demands?|How do fit and crafting complete the picture?|What is the article's conclusion?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What should researchers study next, and what should organizations do?",
 "The article closes by turning its predictions into two lists: Table 2 for research and Table 3 for job design. This is where theory becomes advice you could give an organization.",
 ["The research gaps in Table 2","The design principles in Table 3: human-centred and prospective","Four actions: enhance resources, mitigate demands, promote fit, encourage crafting","The article's conclusion"]),
CARD("Mostly the open issues of step 7 as research questions, plus how technology and context shape demands and resources, and how to make COR measurable.",
 UL([("1","How specific combinations of demands and resources work together (triple-match)."),("2","Whether resources can substitute for each other."),("3","How much and how fast demands and resources must change to affect well-being."),("4","Whether AI acts as a resource or a demand, depending on design and use."),("5","Spillover between work, family and personal life: remote work adds family demands but also flexibility."),("6","Organizational context, such as hierarchical versus flat structures; and, for COR, turning abstract ideas such as caravans into measurable variables.")])+"Table 2 (p. 8) also asks what makes a resource a resource, which new resources matter (digital collaboration tools, psychological safety in remote teams, AI-driven feedback), and how spirals and matching unfold over long periods.",
 "most gaps are the open issues of step 7, turned into research questions.",
 "the principles for design."),
CARD("Four: human-centred, prospective, dynamic and proactive job design.",
 UL([("1","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"humancd\">Human-centred</b>: start from employees' needs, abilities and limits."),("2","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"prospjd\">Prospective</b>: anticipate future demands and resource needs; \"prevention is better than cure\" (p. 9)."),("3","<b>Dynamic</b>: adaptive structures, for example AI redistributing workloads to prevent burnout."),("4","<b>Proactive</b>: involve employees and support job crafting.")])+"The author admits the question remains how such innovative approaches can be managed and practised (p. 9). <i>Our example:</i> asking nurses how a new system will change their day before it goes live, not after complaints.",
 "an exam option saying prospective design means redesigning a job after burnout appears is wrong.",
 "the first action."),
CARD("For example with AI-personalized training and feedback, more agile structures and flexible work arrangements.",
 "Personalized training and feedback build self-efficacy and competence, key personal resources in COR. <b>Self-efficacy</b> is the belief that you can handle what comes. Agile organizations adapt faster and involve employees more, which frees resources. Flexible work arrangements can be both job and personal resources. <i>Our example:</i> a learning app that adjusts exercises to each employee's level.",
 "technology can itself be used to add resources.",
 "the second action."),
CARD("Automate repetitive and physical tasks, make monitoring transparent and give employees a say, and make work timing more predictable.",
 "This is often overlooked because organizations treat demands as given (p. 9). Further measures: ergonomic support, strategic personnel planning and team-based approaches. These address the by-products of step 8. <i>Our example:</i> a hospital introducing software that records each minute nurses spend per patient should explain it openly and let nurses help decide how it is used.",
 "surveillance is a new demand that transparency and voice can soften.",
 "the last two actions."),
CARD("Promote resource fit (the right resource for the demand) and encourage job crafting, for example letting workers choose the AI tools that fit them.",
 "<b>Fit:</b> social support for emotional roles, decision support for cognitive ones, the triple-match principle in practice. <b>Crafting:</b> job crafting interventions have been found to stimulate crafting (Demerouti et al., 2019). <i>Our example:</i> letting each analyst pick the AI assistant that suits her way of working.",
 "Table 3 asks organizations to manage demands, supply matching resources and let employees shape their own jobs.",
 "the conclusion."),
CARD("Both theories highlight the importance of resources in designing future jobs, and research indicates that \"resources become most valuable under challenging conditions\" (Hobfoll &amp; Freedy, 2017; p. 10).",
 "JD-R adds that demands and resources interact: job resources can buffer the impact of demands on strain such as burnout (Demerouti &amp; Bakker, 2023). Future job design should manage demands and provide matching resources.",
 "both theories point the same way: as work gets harder, resources become more, not less, important.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Research the open issues (Table 2); design jobs ahead of time around people, add and match resources, reduce new demands, and let people craft (Table 3).",
 [("Research next?","Combinations, substitution, size and speed of change, AI, spillover, context, measurable COR concepts."),("Design principles?","Human-centred, prospective (prevention is better than cure), dynamic, proactive."),("Add resources?","Personalized training and feedback; agile structures."),("Reduce demands?","Automate repetitive tasks; transparent monitoring with a say; predictable timing."),("Fit and crafting?","Match resources to demands; encourage crafting (Demerouti et al., 2019)."),("Conclusion?","Resources matter for future jobs; they become most valuable under challenging conditions.")],
 ["A hospital introduces minute-by-minute monitoring of nurses. Which implication applies, and what should it do? (mitigate demands: transparency and input)","Which statement is wrong? \"Prospective job design means redesigning after burnout appears.\"","Which implication fits letting workers choose their own AI tools? (encourage job crafting)"])],
 guides=G("<b>Pull the four levers</b>: enhance resources, mitigate demands, promote fit, encourage crafting.","Watch the scale rebalance as you pull each one.","<b>Tap a design card</b> to read the article's example for it."),
 gguide=G("Read the organization's action on the card.","<b>Match it</b> to the implication from Table 3 it best illustrates.","Read why, then go to the next organization."))

def run(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    for n,d in S.items():
        s=apply_step(s,W,n,d['lbl'],d['cards'],guides=d.get('guides'),gguide=d.get('gguide'))
    open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s);print('ok',list(S))
if __name__=='__main__': run(sys.argv[1])
